# The behavioural harness's edge.
#
# Two hosts share one published port. Caddy selects between them on the TLS SNI and
# the Host header, which is how a browser can reach both the API and the web app
# through a single real proxy.
#
# The API site: `localhost` is how the API suite reaches the proxy from the host
# (published as 8443), and `api.marketcore.test` is the public name that stack
# pretends to have. Both go through the same reverse_proxy, so the app's view of the
# request is a real one rather than a header a test set by hand.
#
# The web site is separate rather than routed by path, because the web app and the
# API both serve paths under /api — the BFF's own route handlers and the API's
# `/api/v1` — so one host cannot carry both.
#
# `tls internal` is Caddy's own CA: real TLS termination, with a certificate the
# client is told to trust rather than one a browser would accept. That is what makes
# a `Secure` cookie storable here, which is the whole reason the web suite exists —
# an in-process assertion cannot take that branch, because without TLS the browser
# drops the cookie silently.
https://localhost, https://api.marketcore.test {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy api:3001
}

https://web.marketcore.test {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
